.TH HASHMAP_MATCH 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
hashmap_match \- Check if an entry matches a key and key length

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/hashmap.h>

.IB bool
.IB hashmap_match(hashentry* ent, const char* key, size_t keylen);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBhashmap_match\fR function checks if an entry matches a given key and key length. It takes a pointer to the hashentry, the key, and the length of the key as arguments.

The function takes the following parameters:
.IB hashentry*
A pointer to the hashentry to be checked.
.IB const char*
The key to be compared.
.IB size_t
The length of the key.

The function returns true if the entry matches the key and key length, and false otherwise.

The \fBhashmap_match\fR function is intended to be used internally by the hashmap implementation to determine if an entry corresponds to a given key.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBhashmap_match\fR function to check if an entry matches a key and key length:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/hashmap.h>

int main() {
    hashmap* map = hashmap_new();
    const char* key = "example";

    hashmap_set(map, key, "value");

    hashentry* entry = hashmap_get_entry(map, key, 7);

    if (entry && hashmap_match(entry, key, 7)) {
        printf("Entry matches key\n");
    } else {
        printf("Entry does not match key\n");
    }

    hashmap_free(map);

    return 0;
}
.nef